# The literature wiki

Open this when you add a source, write or update an article, or look something up in the
literature. The wiki starts at [wiki/_index.md](../../wiki/_index.md); terms are in
[GLOSSARY.md](../../GLOSSARY.md).

## Why a wiki

Papers read in one session are forgotten by the next unless what mattered is written down
where the next reader will find it. The wiki separates three things that get muddled
otherwise: what a source says (`raw/`), what we make of it (`concepts/`, `references/`),
and what we still have to read (`inbox/`).

## Rules

**Read the index before scanning.** Every folder has an `_index.md` listing its files.
Start from `wiki/_index.md`, then the folder's index; open individual files only once the
index points you there. When you add a file, add its row to the folder's index in the same
change.
*Why:* scanning a folder file by file is slow and misses context the index gives; an index
that is not kept current is worse than none.
*Anti-scope:* a text search across the wiki for an exact term is fine and often faster.

**Raw is immutable.** A file in `wiki/raw/` records a source as ingested and is never
edited afterwards. If the extraction was bad, ingest it again as a new file and say so in
the log.
*Why:* when an article and its source disagree, the source has to be the fixed point;
otherwise errors drift into both.
*Anti-scope:* binaries and sources that cannot be committed (license, size) get a pointer
file in `raw/` instead of a copy, with `kind: pointer`, a `pointer-reason:`, and short
quotes of every statement the project uses from it. A copy for reading may sit in
`wiki/raw/local/`, which git ignores; the pointer and its quotes stay the record.

**Ingest a source the moment it enters the project, and synthesize it before working off
it.** Follow the `ingest` skill: the raw record, then what you take from the source in an
article in `concepts/` (the statement with its hypotheses, the idea of the proof or method,
and how it connects to what the project already has){{#if modules.known_results}}, then its results about our objects
in `known-results/`{{/if}}. A `references/` page may list and compare sources, but it does not
replace the article. `bin/wiki-check` lists raw files nothing synthesizes.
*Why:* writing the synthesis is where a misread hypothesis or a scope mismatch shows up,
before it costs an experiment. The article is also what the next session reads instead of
the paper; a source left as a link (in `raw/` or in a notes file) is read again by every
session that needs it, or not at all.
*Anti-scope:* looking up a single value or definition does not need an article; cite the
raw file. Nor does a source used for one stated result: the raw record quoting that result
with its hypotheses and page{{#if modules.known_results}}, plus its entry in `known-results/`, is enough{{else}} is enough, with the file named in `.wikiignore` ("one result, quoted"){{/if}}.
Write the article once the project uses the source's method or a second of its results.

**Articles are synthesized, not copied.** An article explains an idea in the project's
terms, draws on one or more sources, and says how confident it is, with the reason on the
same line (`confidence: medium — one preprint, not refereed; the key lemma's proof is a
sketch`; the word is `high`, `medium` or `low`, from the quality of the sources). It lists
its raw sources under `## Sources`.
*Why:* a pasted abstract adds nothing the raw file did not have; a grade without its reason
reads later as a verdict on the mathematics, and nobody can check it.
*Anti-scope:* a short verbatim quote of a theorem statement is fine, marked as a quote.

**Link both ways, with ordinary links.** Cross-references between wiki pages are ordinary
relative markdown links, such as `[Literature wiki](literature-wiki.md)`. When page A links
page B under `## See also`, B links A back.
*Why:* agents, code hosts and the link checker follow markdown links; the link back lets a
reader arriving at B find what builds on it.
*Anti-scope:* the conjecture and question links `[[C001-slug]]` and `[[Q001-slug]]` keep
their own form.

**Log every operation.** Append one entry to `wiki/log.md` per ingest, synthesis, or
reorganization: `## [YYYY-MM-DD] operation | description`. Never edit an existing entry.
*Why:* the log answers "when did we learn this, and from what?" after the fact.
*Anti-scope:* reading the wiki without changing it is not an operation.

**Say when the wiki has no answer.** If the wiki does not cover a question, say so and name
what should be read, rather than answering from memory as if it came from a source. Add the
source to the inbox.
{{#if modules.firsthand}}
For an external search, use the `lit-search` agent; its theorem attributions are readings
to check against the source before they enter the wiki.
{{/if}}
*Why:* an answer that looks sourced but is not is the hardest error to find later.
*Anti-scope:* standard background (textbook material) can be stated without a wiki source,
but name a textbook or standard reference where it can be found (a book and section). A
result you cannot place in one is not background.

**Work the inbox one item at a time.** An inbox item is one source, with why it is there.
Ingest it to `raw/`, synthesize it, {{#if modules.known_results}}file its results about
objects we study in `wiki/known-results/`, {{/if}}log it, and delete the inbox note. Never delete the raw copy.
*Why:* an inbox that only grows becomes a reading list nobody reads.
*Anti-scope:* an inbox note can be deleted without ingesting it if it turns out not to bear on
the project; log that decision.
